config WOLFSSL_ECC
  bool "wolfCrypt ECC support"
  depends on !WOLFSSL_HAS_SETTINGS_FILE
  default y
  help
    Enable ECC (HAVE_ECC). At least one curve must be selected below - a
    build with none is rejected, because wolfCrypt has no curve table to
    size its types from.

if WOLFSSL_ECC

config WOLFSSL_ECC_192
  bool "SECP192R1 (P-192)"
  help
    Enable the NIST P-192 curve (HAVE_ECC192). SP math has no implementation
    at this size, so selecting it forces the generic SP variant.

    P-192 is below every current recommendation and is offered for
    interoperability with existing deployments only. Selecting it also lowers
    WOLFSSL_ECC_MIN_KEY_SZ to 192, because wolfCrypt filters curves smaller
    than that minimum out of its curve table and the option would otherwise
    appear to do nothing.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_224
  bool "SECP224R1 (P-224)"
  help
    Enable the NIST P-224 curve (HAVE_ECC224). SP math has no implementation
    at this size, so selecting it forces the generic SP variant.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_256
  bool "SECP256R1 (P-256)"
  default y
  help
    Enable the NIST P-256 curve. This is the curve TLS uses by default and
    the only one the NXP EdgeLock key store can hold, so leave it on unless
    you know the whole application avoids it.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_384
  bool "SECP384R1 (P-384)"
  help
    Enable the NIST P-384 curve (HAVE_ECC384 and the SP implementation
    behind WOLFSSL_SP_384).

    Enabling a curve larger than P-256 raises MAX_ECC_BYTES, so every ECC
    key, signature buffer and temporary grows accordingly - including on the
    stack. Size thread stacks for the largest curve selected.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_320
  bool "320-bit curves"
  help
    Enable the 320-bit curve size (HAVE_ECC320). There is no NIST curve at
    this size - it exists for brainpoolP320r1, so it is only useful together
    with WOLFSSL_ECC_BRAINPOOL.

    SP math has no implementation at this size, so selecting it moves the
    build onto the generic variant; see WOLFSSL_ECC_KOBLITZ for what that
    costs.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_512
  bool "512-bit curves"
  help
    Enable the 512-bit curve size (HAVE_ECC512). As with WOLFSSL_ECC_320
    there is no NIST curve at this size; it exists for brainpoolP512r1, and
    it likewise forces the generic SP variant.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_521
  bool "SECP521R1 (P-521)"
  help
    Enable the NIST P-521 curve (HAVE_ECC521 and the SP implementation
    behind WOLFSSL_SP_521). This sets the largest MAX_ECC_BYTES of any
    supported curve.

    Its SP implementation is also the most stack-hungry: a thread signing on
    P-521 overflowed a 16 KB stack during bring-up and needed roughly three
    times that. Raise CONFIG_MAIN_STACK_SIZE and any relevant thread stack
    accordingly.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_KOBLITZ
  bool "Koblitz curves (secp224k1, secp256k1)"
  select WOLFSSL_ECC_521
  help
    Enable the Koblitz curve variants (HAVE_ECC_KOBLITZ), including
    secp256k1.

    Each Koblitz curve additionally requires its matching size to be
    enabled: secp256k1 needs WOLFSSL_ECC_256 and secp224k1 needs
    WOLFSSL_ECC_224. Selecting this alone adds nothing. The 160-bit and
    192-bit Koblitz curves are not reachable from this menu, which offers no
    160-bit size and filters 192 out through WOLFSSL_ECC_MIN_KEY_SZ.

    Implies WOLFSSL_CUSTOM_CURVES, which wolfCrypt requires for any
    non-prime-field curve.

    Selects WOLFSSL_ECC_521 to lift wolfCrypt's bignum ceiling. Every curve in
    this family lands exactly on a 64-bit digit boundary, and the generic path
    needs more than the modulus for its intermediates, so an exact fit fails at
    runtime with a key-size error. The ceiling comes from MAX_ECC_BITS_NEEDED,
    computed from the enabled HAVE_ECCxxx set alone, so no other knob raises
    it. The cost is P-521's MAX_ECC_BITS and stack appetite, build-wide.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_BRAINPOOL
  bool "Brainpool curves"
  select WOLFSSL_ECC_521
  help
    Enable the Brainpool curve family (HAVE_ECC_BRAINPOOL). As with the
    Koblitz curves, each one also needs its matching size enabled, and this
    likewise implies WOLFSSL_CUSTOM_CURVES.

    brainpoolP256r1 needs WOLFSSL_ECC_256, brainpoolP320r1 needs
    WOLFSSL_ECC_320, brainpoolP384r1 needs WOLFSSL_ECC_384 and
    brainpoolP512r1 needs WOLFSSL_ECC_512. The 320 and 512 sizes exist for
    these curves alone.

    Selects WOLFSSL_ECC_521 for the same reason, and at the same cost, as
    WOLFSSL_ECC_KOBLITZ above.

config WOLFSSL_ECC_MIN_KEY_SZ
  int "Minimum accepted ECC key size (bits)"
  default 192 if WOLFSSL_ECC_192
  default 224
  range 112 520
  help
    Sets WOLFSSL_MIN_ECC_BITS. This has two effects, and the second is easy
    to miss.

    It drops any curve smaller than this from wolfCrypt's table, so it must
    be no larger than the smallest curve enabled above or that curve silently
    disappears.

    It is also the minimum ECC key the TLS layer will accept from a peer, so
    lowering it widens which peer certificates the stack considers
    acceptable - a security decision, not just a curve-availability one.

    Must be a multiple of 8; wolfSSL rejects anything else with a hard build
    error from inside internal.h. The range stops short of 521 for that
    reason - P-521 keys are accepted under a 520-bit minimum.

endif # WOLFSSL_ECC

